  "answer": "Urban young women (15‑29) have a much lower labour force participation than the broader urban female population – 23.2 % in 2025 (annual) and 22.1 % in September 2026 (monthly) versus an overall urban female LFPR of 27.7 % in 2025. Their unemployment rate is also higher: urban female youth unemployment was 6.7 % in 2025 (annual) and 5.5 % in September 2026, compared with 4.5 % for urban males and 5.2 % overall urban unemployment in September 2026. The gap shows that young women in cities are both less likely to be in the labour force and more likely to be unemployed when they are in it.",
